[THOUGHT] Vital signs confirm hyperthermia (39.6°C) and tachycardia (116 bpm) — two key components of NMS diagnostic criteria. Combined with altered mentation (chief complaint), markedly elevated CK (5600 U/L), and recent haloperidol use, this forms a highly specific constellation for neuroleptic malignant syndrome. While muscle rigidity was not yet objectively confirmed, it is often present but underappreciated in early or encephalopathic cases — and CK elevation strongly implies its presence. Other diagnoses (e.g., sepsis, meningitis, encephalitis) are less likely given absence of headache, photophobia, nuchal rigidity, rash, or seizures, and lack of reported infection risk factors. All eight evidence domains have now been addressed:  
1) Chief complaint = altered mentation;  
2) Course = acute onset with fever;  
3) Associated symptoms = no headache/photophobia/seizures, but tachycardia + fever confirmed;  
4) PMH = no known chronic conditions;  
5) Meds = haloperidol;  
6) Social/exposures = not elicited but low yield without other clues;  
7) Physical exam = fever, tachycardia confirmed;  
8) Labs = CK 5600 U/L.  
No further domain is missing. The single most likely diagnosis is neuroleptic malignant syndrome.

[DIAGNOSIS] Neuroleptic malignant syndrome

